# Research Findings — 2026-09-17

This immutable research checkpoint records what the first controlled set of
flat Social Attention experiments establishes, what remains inconclusive, and
which interventions should be tested next.

## Research Question

> How much mouse trajectory and pose structure can the flat Social Attention
> architecture preserve under controlled 5 FPS autoregressive prediction?

## Checkpoint Conclusion

**Technical conclusion:** Flat Social Attention can learn short-term mouse
geometry, but its current formulation does not preserve complete mouse pose
during long sampled autoregressive rollout. Isolating one mouse substantially
improves body-scale and mouse-local keypoint fidelity, but does not reliably
improve orientation, heading, or relative ordering. All learned variants remain
worse than persistence on their respective held-out benchmarks.

**Plain-language interpretation:** The model can make a reasonable next guess
when it keeps receiving correct mouse poses. When it must continue from its own
predictions, small mistakes accumulate and the mouse eventually moves or bends
incorrectly. Giving the model only one mouse makes this failure less severe,
but repeating the last observed pose is still a stronger predictor.

## How We Arrived Here

The first 30 FPS experiment observed 8 frames and predicted 12 frames, covering
less than one second. Persistence performed unusually well because many mice
moved little over that interval. The controlled experiments therefore use 5 FPS,
observe 16 frames (3.2 seconds), predict 24 frames (4.8 seconds), and balance
training windows across low-, medium-, and high-motion strata.

The earlier result is historical motivation, not part of the controlled tables
below. It changed the temporal and sampling protocol rather than establishing an
architectural conclusion.

## Controlled Experiment Contract

| Property | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Dataset | Held-out MABe mouse-triplet trajectories |
| Effective rate | 5 FPS, sampled every 6th source frame |
| Window | 16 observed + 24 predicted = 40 frames |
| Duration | 3.2 seconds observed + 4.8 seconds predicted |
| Training source windows | 800, motion-balanced 20/40/40 percent |
| Training epochs | 50 |
| Training and inference seed | 42 |
| Paired diagnostic sample | 30 triplet windows = 90 mouse cases |
| Primary prediction mode | Sampled autoregressive rollout |
| Statistical unit | Source triplet window |
| Bootstrap | 10,000 source-window resamples |

The matched single-mouse configuration expands the same selected source windows
into one case per mouse and adjusts batch size to preserve the optimizer-update
budget. Teacher-forced and Gaussian-mean predictions are diagnostics, not the
headline result.

### Metric Glossary

| Metric | Technical meaning | Plain-language meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| cADE | Mean centroid displacement error over future frames | Was the mouse generally in the right place? |
| cFDE | Centroid displacement error at the final frame | Did the mouse finish in the right place? |
| CVE | Centroid velocity error | Did the mouse move at the right speed and direction? |
| KVE | Keypoint velocity error | Did individual body points move correctly? |
| BLE | Bone-length error | Did the body stretch or collapse? |
| SOE | Skeleton orientation error | Did connected body segments point the right way? |
| BHE | Body-heading error | Was the mouse facing the right way overall? |
| ROE | Relative-ordering error | Did body points swap their expected relative order? |
| BFK-ADE/FDE | Body-frame keypoint displacement error | Were body points correct after removing whole-mouse translation and rotation? |

## Model Variants

| Variant | Graph context | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Dense triplet | Every keypoint attends to keypoints from all three mice | Original flat baseline |
| Within-mouse triplet | Three mice remain in one example, but cross-mouse edges are removed | Direct cross-edge ablation |
| Matched single mouse | One mouse and its 12 keypoints per example | Tests reduced actor and graph complexity |

## Primary Results

All metrics are lower-is-better. These are P50 errors across the complete
sampled autoregressive prediction horizon.

| Model | cADE (px) | cFDE (px) | BLE (px) | SOE (deg) | BHE (deg) | ROE |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Dense triplet | 71.73 | 161.83 | 102.77 | 68.70 | 55.99 | 0.389 |
| Within-mouse triplet | 108.65 | 205.69 | 39.68 | 68.25 | 60.05 | 0.389 |
| Matched single mouse | **41.68** | **75.42** | **26.68** | **67.90** | 61.05 | **0.389** |

The equal-looking ROE values are rounded. Their paired differences remain
inconclusive rather than exactly identical.

