## Findings and Interpretations

### 1. Body scale is the clearest learned difference

**Technical finding:** Both variants without cross-mouse edges have markedly
lower BLE than the dense graph. The matched single-mouse model also improves
body-frame keypoint displacement.

**Plain-language interpretation:** The dense model often stretches or collapses
the mouse. Removing other mice from a keypoint's attention makes it easier to
keep body parts at approximately the right distances. Showing only one mouse
also helps keep those parts near their correct mouse-local positions.

### 2. Better BLE does not mean complete pose understanding

**Technical finding:** SOE, BHE, and ROE do not improve reliably for either
no-cross-mouse-edge variant. Their paired confidence intervals include zero.

**Plain-language interpretation:** A mouse can have roughly correct body-part
distances while still facing the wrong direction, twisting incorrectly, or
placing keypoints in the wrong relative order. The model has improved one part
of anatomy, not learned the whole pose.

### 3. The single-mouse gain is not only a cross-edge effect

**Technical finding:** Within-mouse triplet and single-mouse graphs both remove
cross-mouse edges, but single mouse has lower paired trajectory, velocity, BLE,
and body-frame displacement errors with confidence intervals excluding zero.

**Plain-language interpretation:** Deleting connections between mice is not
enough. Keeping three separate mice alive in the same recurrent prediction is
itself difficult. Giving the model one actor reduces that burden.

### 4. Autoregressive rollout is the dominant failure mode

Teacher-forced Gaussian-mean P50 errors remain small for all variants: BLE is
about 4 px, SOE about 9 degrees, and ROE about 0.06. Those errors deteriorate
sharply when the model consumes its own sampled predictions.

**Plain-language interpretation:** The model behaves well when every previous
answer is corrected by a teacher. When no teacher corrects it, each imperfect
answer becomes the input for the next answer and the mistakes compound.

### 5. Predicted uncertainty is not trustworthy yet

At the final future frame, nominal 95% Gaussian coverage is 5.2% for dense,
4.4% for within-mouse triplet, and 12.9% for single mouse.

**Plain-language interpretation:** When the model says “the real point should
almost certainly be inside this region,” it is usually outside. Single mouse is
less overconfident, but none of the models provides calibrated uncertainty.

### 6. The best learned model still loses to persistence

On its 1,000-window benchmark, matched single mouse has P50 cADE 34.36 px, BLE
27.12 px, SOE 66.98 degrees, and ROE 0.378. Repeating the final observed pose
achieves 6.64 px, 7.21 px, 23.45 degrees, and 0.150 respectively.

**Plain-language interpretation:** Single mouse is the best learned variant so
far, but it is not yet a useful forecaster. A model that assumes the mouse never
moves is still more accurate over this evaluation set.

## Unsupported or Inconclusive Hypotheses

- **“Removing cross-mouse edges improves ROE.”** Not supported. Both paired ROE
  intervals include zero.
- **“Better validation NLL guarantees a better rollout.”** Rejected for these
  checkpoints. Within-mouse triplet has the best validation NLL but the worst
  sampled trajectory and velocity errors.
- **“Cross-mouse edges alone cause pose failure.”** Not established. They affect
  BLE, but SOE, BHE, and ROE remain poor after removing them.
- **“The flat architecture has no pose capacity.”** Too strong. Teacher-forced
  predictions show useful one-step capacity; maintaining it during rollout is
  the unresolved problem.

## Limitations

- Each model has one training run with seed 42.
- The paired confidence intervals measure held-out-window variability, not
  variation between retrained models.
- The stochastic diagnostic uses one rollout seed.
- Thirty source windows support diagnosis, not a universal architectural claim.
- This checkpoint evaluates trajectory reconstruction, not whether the learned
  representation supports downstream behavioral classification.

## Next Controlled Experiments

Run each intervention independently before combining them.

### A. Auxiliary angle-loss ablation

**Technical hypothesis:** Penalizing anatomical edge-angle error during training
may improve SOE and BHE, and possibly ROE, without materially worsening
trajectory accuracy.

**Plain-language hypothesis:** The model currently gets punished when a point is
far away, but not directly when the mouse is twisted. Add a penalty that tells
it when connected body parts point in the wrong directions.

Compare the matched single-mouse control against the same model with only the
auxiliary angle loss changed. Success requires paired SOE or BHE improvement,
with no material cADE, cFDE, BLE, or velocity regression.

### B. Scheduled-sampling ablation

**Technical hypothesis:** Gradually replacing ground-truth recurrent inputs with
model-generated inputs during training may reduce exposure bias and the gap
between teacher-forced and autoregressive metrics.

**Plain-language hypothesis:** Let the model practice continuing from its own
mistakes during training, instead of showing it a perfect previous frame every
time.

Keep the architecture and loss unchanged. Success requires a smaller
teacher-forced-to-autoregressive gap and paired improvements in sampled rollout
metrics.

### C. Conditional combined experiment

Run angle loss and scheduled sampling together only if at least one isolated
ablation helps. This preserves attribution before testing whether their gains
are complementary.

**Plain-language plan:** First learn whether each idea works alone. Combine them
only after we know which ingredient is useful.

### D. Optional centroid–pose factorization

**Technical hypothesis:** Predicting arena-space centroid motion separately from
mouse-local keypoint offsets may disentangle translation from articulated pose.
Reconstruct global keypoints from the predicted centroid, heading, and local
pose.

**Plain-language hypothesis:** Stop asking one output to answer “Where is the
mouse?” and “What shape is its body?” simultaneously. Predict movement with one
part and body arrangement with another, then put them back together.

This is optional because it changes the representation and architecture more
substantially than the first two ablations.

